# {{project_name}} — Execution protocol ({{initiative_title}})

> **What this is.** The operating manual of the build. It defines HOW the catalog in
> `detailed-plan.md` gets executed: **wave sessions** (not 1 task = 1 session),
> self-verified loops with the **DoD-auto as the exit criterion**, workflow fan-out
> where parallelism helps, and **mandatory adversarial verification at ⚠gates**. The
> copy-paste prompts per wave are at the end (§7). Keep in sync with `task.md`
> (state) and `master-plan.md` (rationale). Prompts are tuned for Fable at high
> effort but run on any model.

## 1. Principles

1. **Session = one wave: 1–4 coupled tasks, one branch/MR per wave.**
2. **The loop ends by DoD, not by fatigue.** Each wave defines its **DoD-auto**: a
   list 100% verifiable by command. While it isn't green the loop continues:
   implement → verify → fix. Max 5 rounds; if the SAME failure doesn't converge in
   **3 attempts** → stop, report, **never degrade tests to pass**.
3. **DoD-auto ≠ DoD-human.** Actions only {{owner}} can take (dashboards, DNS,
   purchases, approvals) live in the **DoD-human**: when the DoD-auto is exhausted
   the task is marked `blocked` with the human checklist rendered at close. It only
   becomes `completed` when {{owner}} confirms.
4. **Implementers never self-approve at gates.** On ⚠gate waves the executor is an
   `opus` sub-agent and the loop does NOT close without **adversarial
   verification** by ANOTHER `opus` sub-agent (the reserved one — never the
   executor itself, never the orchestrator's tier) that did NOT see the
   implementation, given only the diff + the DoD + that wave's attack checklist (§6).
5. **Inviolable decisions** (no session may contradict them — from
   `master-plan.md` §8): {{list_them_inline}}.
6. **No hardcoded dates**: the logbook uses the date of the day of execution.
7. **Agent budget 20 with fixed roles.** The main session (Fable) ORCHESTRATES:
   it decides design and scope, audits the deliverables `opus` investigators return
   (solution · files · considerations · open questions), writes one executor brief
   per task, runs every DoD-slice and the full DoD-auto itself, and integrates —
   it never implements first and never spawns its own tier. `opus` sub-agents
   investigate, review regression and verify (quota 10 per session, 1 reserved for
   the ⚠gate verifier; audits pass to `sonnet` once spent) and are the executors
   on ⚠gate waves. `sonnet` sub-agents implement NO-gate tasks from briefs, ≤4 in
   parallel with disjoint files in their own worktrees, and never self-approve.
   **Escalation ladder** Sonnet → Opus → orchestrator: the 2nd failure of the SAME
   DoD line moves the task one tier up as a new agent carrying the attempt log.
   One counter per session, `model` always explicit, `SendMessage` to a live agent
   costs 0. Ultracode changes the orchestration (Workflow per phase + adversarial
   cross-check), never roles or count. Exceeding 20 is a protocol violation; the
   logbook records `agents used: n/20 (opus a · sonnet b)` and every escalation.
8. **Executor brief.** What the orchestrator hands to an executor, ≤40 lines:
   `TASK · FILES (from the Scope manifest) · DELIVERABLE (Opus's audited deliverable
   with the orchestrator's decisions) · DESIGN DECISIONS · DOD-SLICE (command →
   expected) · STOP IF (stop and report instead of improvising) · FORBIDDEN ·
   RETURN (diff summary per file · commands + output · open questions)`. Every §7
   prompt carries one pre-filled block per task. A poor brief produces poor code.

